<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Closing: the overlay goes back down off the bottom edge, which is how
    Chrome's custom tab closes and is a different statement from the one the
    ENTRANCE makes on purpose.

    Arriving, a full-height slide would be wrong — it is Android's way of
    saying "you have gone somewhere else", and this window is meant to say
    "this is on top of what you were doing" — so it fades up a finger's width
    and settles (custom_tab_enter). Leaving is the opposite situation: there
    is nothing to settle onto and nothing to be careful about claiming, the
    app underneath is already drawn and is where the user is going. Sliding
    the whole thing off the bottom says the page has been PUT DOWN, and it
    leaves the caller uncovered progressively rather than dissolving over it,
    which is what a fade at this size actually looks like — a brief moment of
    two apps mixed together.

    It is also the half of the back gesture that can only be drawn by the
    platform: by the time this runs, a swipe-back has already shrunk the
    window to 90% under the finger (see CustomTabScreen's PredictiveBackHandler),
    so what leaves is a card being let go of rather than a screen being torn
    away.

    No alpha: a slide that also fades reads as two things happening to one
    window. `Accelerate` from ui/Motion.kt — an exit leaves rather than eases.
-->
<translate xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:fromYDelta="0"
    android:toYDelta="100%p"
    android:duration="250"
    android:interpolator="@interpolator/accelerate_leave" />
